WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.799
Make a good GIF from a video clip.

2
00:00:04.299 --> 00:00:08.344
Why GIFs get huge, and the four settings that fix it: width, frame rate,

3
00:00:08.344 --> 00:00:12.446
length and colours. Includes a planner that estimates the size before you

4
00:00:12.446 --> 00:00:12.896
convert.

5
00:00:14.296 --> 00:00:19.371
Why GIFs get so big. A video codec like H.264 stores one full picture now

6
00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:24.237
and then and, between them, only the changes, using motion prediction.

7
00:00:25.537 --> 00:00:27.299
Step 2: pick sensible settings.

8
00:00:27.299 --> 00:00:31.105
This matters most because pixels grow with the square of the width.

9
00:00:32.405 --> 00:00:34.600
Step 3: make it smaller after the fact.

10
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.597
If the result is still too large for where it is going (Discord, GitHub

11
00:00:38.597 --> 00:00:42.368
and many forums have upload limits), run it through GIF Compressor.

12
00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:45.704
My recipe for a bug report GIF.

13
00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:50.563
It usually lands between 1 and 4 MB, the text stays readable, and it plays

14
00:00:50.563 --> 00:00:52.073
right inside the issue.

15
00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:55.897
To try it, open Video to GIF in OpenFile.

16
00:00:55.897 --> 00:00:59.407
Drop in a file, adjust the settings and press the button.

17
00:00:59.407 --> 00:01:02.547
It all runs on your device, so nothing is uploaded.

18
00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:09.951
Read the full post, with live demos, on the OpenFile blog.
